# Code 54 under low I/O: write the accumulated solar right ascension, not the
# instantaneous one.
#
# WHAT IT CHANGES. One line, in the `nlowio > 0` branch of `outsc` in
# `outmod.f90`. Nothing else.
#
# WHAT IS WRONG. That branch writes five orbital scalars, codes 50 to 54, and
# four of them follow the same two-line shape: divide the accumulator by
# `naccuout`, then write the accumulator.
#
#     aorbnu = aorbnu/real(max(1,naccuout))
#     call writescalar(40,aorbnu*180./PI,50)
#     ...
#     ardist = ardist/real(max(1,naccuout))
#     call writescalar(40,ardist,53)
#
# The fifth divides `arasc` and then writes `rasc`. The averaged value is
# computed and discarded, and the instantaneous right ascension at the output
# step goes out under code 54 instead. `arasc` is zeroed in `outreset` and
# summed in `outaccu` exactly like its four siblings, so the accumulator is
# correct and only the write is wrong; this is a copy-paste slip in upstream
# 3.4.2, not a design choice. The three other `writescalar(...,54)` sites
# (`outsc`'s `nlowio == 0` branch, `snapshotsc`, `hcadencesc`) are instantaneous
# streams by definition and correctly write `rasc`; they are untouched here.
#
# WHAT HAPPENS IF IT IS ABSENT. Code 54 in `MOST.NNNNN.nc` from a run with
# `NLOWIO = 1` is a sample rather than an interval mean, while codes 50 to 53
# beside it are interval means. It is mislabelled rather than noisy: `rasc`
# advances monotonically through the orbit, so the sampled value leads the mean
# by about half an output interval, and the two agree only in the limit of one
# accumulation step. Nothing under `NLOWIO = 0` is affected, and no field other
# than code 54 is affected under either. As of 2026-08-18 the only reader of
# code 54 in this repository is `exoplasim/scripts/build_climatology.py`, which
# carries `rasc` through as an orbital-geometry variable without averaging it and
# requires it to agree across the orbits it merges; a mislabelled code 54 is
# therefore a wrong number in a climatology attribute-like field rather than a
# wrong climate. That is why this is worth keeping and not worth hurrying.
#
# THE BASE IT APPLIES TO. Authored 2026-08-18 against the vendored source with
# thirteen resident patches applied and `exoplasim-3.4.2-lowio-first-record.patch`
# NOT applied:
#
#     outmod.f90, this base                       9055e2e905ee3855e83949ffb68b833b060a92cde150c9f6e0b90cebc43eaddf
#
# `exoplasim-3.4.2-denergy-accumulator.patch` is the only other resident patch
# that touches `outmod.f90`, and all three of its hunks sit below this one, so
# the hunk here is byte-identical to what it would be against pristine 3.4.2 and
# applies there too:
#
#     outmod.f90, pristine 3.4.2                  2f582ee248ca70c7feee759ea723b8de327fdac2cfc0234fd8ce351240d79364
#
# Verify before applying, and refuse if it matches neither:
#
#     sha256sum .venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/exoplasim/plasim/src/outmod.f90
#     patch --forward --strip=1 --directory=.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/exoplasim/plasim/src < exoplasim/patches/exoplasim-3.4.2-arasc-output.patch
#
# WHY IT IS ITS OWN PATCH. Split out of
# `exoplasim-3.4.2-lowio-first-record.patch` on 2026-08-18, when that patch was
# dropped from `RESIDENT_PATCHES`. The reason recorded at the time was that the
# parent tripled model runtime under gcc-fortran 16.2.1. THAT WAS WRONG. The
# compiler is not involved at all: the same orbit takes 77.7 s built with 16.1.1
# and 78.2 s with 16.2.1. What the parent actually did was DEADLOCK the model at
# `NLOWIO = 0`, by making a per-timestep condition depend on `nlowio`, which was
# never broadcast to the non-root tasks. See
# `notes/audits/nlowio-collective-deadlock.md`. The parent is resident again,
# with `exoplasim-3.4.2-nlowio-broadcast.patch` under it.
#
# The split is kept anyway, on the merits it should have been made on: this is a
# one-line output-labelling slip in `outsc`, the parent repairs a restart
# accumulator defect, and they are different bugs touching different files.
# Apart, either can be reverted or re-based without disturbing the other.
#
# IF YOU ARE COMPARING THE TWO: until 2026-08-18 the parent still carried a
# byte-identical copy of the hunk below, so the split left a DUPLICATE and the
# two patches could not both apply. The hunk was removed from the parent and
# this file owns it. Neither patch should ever contain it twice again.
#
# The independence is the point and it is a constraint on this file, not a
# description of it. This patch touches `outmod.f90` only. It must not touch
# `plasim.f90`, must not touch `make_plasim`, and must not introduce a module
# dependency, because those three are what made the parent patch expensive. It
# adds no namelist key, changes no restart record, and changes no field a binary
# built without it would write differently at `NLOWIO = 0`, so a restart written
# by an unpatched binary is readable by a patched one and vice versa.
#
# WHAT MUST LAND WITH IT. It is one line and still obeys CLAUDE.md rule 4:
#
#   1. add it to `RESIDENT_PATCHES` in `exoplasim/scripts/rebuild_binaries.py`
#   2. apply it, then `python exoplasim/scripts/rebuild_binaries.py`, then
#      `--verify`
#   3. `outmod.f90` is not `radmod.f90`, so the star-cycle base pin does not move
#
--- a/outmod.f90
+++ b/outmod.f90
@@ -271,7 +271,7 @@
         
         !Solar right ascension
         arasc = arasc/real(max(1,naccuout))
-        call writescalar(40,rasc*180./PI,54)
+        call writescalar(40,arasc*180./PI,54)
       endif
       
       return
